of an acidic amino acid?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystine"},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrosine"},{"label":"C","text":"Glutamine"},{"label":"D","text":"Serine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[],"manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-e48bbe6bf715","stem":"Choose the correct category for milk protein casein out of the followings","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nucleoprotein"},{"label":"B","text":"Phospho protein"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipoprotein"},{"label":"D","text":"Glycoprotein Answers-"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2f105ea8162c","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a substrate for gluconeogenesis? At which complex of the oxidative phosphorylation pathway does ATP synthesis occur? What does covalent modification mean in terms of enzyme activity regulation? and pancreas? Which of the following glucose transporters is mostly found in insulin sensitive tissues? Which of the following enzymes is activated by NADH and Acetyl-coA? During starvation which amino acids prevail in circulation? What is the GROSS ATP production during anaerobic glycolysis per one mole glucose? haemoglobin’s affinity for oxygen? which compartment of the cell? Which of the following tissues depend on insulin for glucose uptake? with eventual secretion of insulin? three inhibitors? following as a route cause? anaerobic glycolysis? Which of the following chemicals is an allosteric effector of phosphofrucokinase-1? fatty acids, glucose, ethanol and amino acids starting with the fastest one? membrane from matrix to the cytosol? Reactive oxygen species can result from all of the following processes except which one?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lactate"},{"label":"B","text":"Glycerol"},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvate"},{"label":"D","text":"Acetyl-coA"},{"label":"A","text":"Complex I"},{"label":"B","text":"Complex II"},{"label":"C","text":"Complex III"},{"label":"D","text":"Complex V"},{"label":"A","text":"Formation of covalent bond by sharing of electrons"},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphorylation / dephosphorylation strategy"},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphorylation strategy"},{"label":"D","text":"Dephosphorylation strategy Which of the following glucose transporters is mostly found in kidney, liver, intestine"},{"label":"A","text":"GLUT-2"},{"label":"B","text":"GLUT-3"},{"label":"C","text":"GLUT-4"},{"label":"D","text":"GLUT-5"},{"label":"A","text":"GLUT-2"},{"label":"B","text":"GLUT-3"},{"label":"C","text":"GLUT-4"},{"label":"D","text":"GLUT-5"},{"label":"A","text":"PDH-complex"},{"label":"B","text":"PDH-kinase"},{"label":"C","text":"PDH-phosphatase"},{"label":"D","text":"Lipoyl transacetylase"},{"label":"A","text":"Alanine"},{"label":"B","text":"Glutamine"},{"label":"C","text":"Alanine and glutamine"},{"label":"D","text":"Alanine and asparagines Usually important control points of a reaction pathway are:"},{"label":"A","text":"the irreversible paths"},{"label":"B","text":"the reversible paths"},{"label":"C","text":"the high energy reactions"},{"label":"D","text":"low energy reactions"},{"label":"A","text":"10 ATPs"},{"label":"B","text":"8 ATPs"},{"label":"C","text":"6 ATPs"},{"label":"D","text":"4 ATPs Which of the following intermediate products of glycolysis is a regulator of"},{"label":"A","text":"2,3-diphosphoglcerate"},{"label":"B","text":"1,3-bisphosphoglycerate"},{"label":"C","text":"fructose 2,3-bisphosphate"},{"label":"D","text":"3-phosphoglyceraldehyde During gluconeogenesis, the reaction catalyzed by pyruvate carboxylase takes place in"},{"label":"A","text":"Mitochondria"},{"label":"B","text":"Endoplasmic reticulum"},{"label":"C","text":"Cytosol"},{"label":"D","text":"Nucleus Even at very low blood glucose levels brain tissue can still get significant amount of glucose for energy. The reason is:"},{"label":"A","text":"Brain has a low affinity glucokinase that saturates easily with glucose."},{"label":"B","text":"Brain has a high affinity hexokinase that binds glucose and saturates at low concentrations."},{"label":"C","text":"Brain has no ability to dephosphorylate Glucose-1-P"},{"label":"D","text":"Brain tissue is able to convert fatty acids to glucose."},{"label":"A","text":"liver"},{"label":"B","text":"skeletal muscle"},{"label":"C","text":"adipose tissue"},{"label":"D","text":"both adipose and skeletal muscle tissues Which of the above glucose transporters mediates glucose uptake by pancreatic beta cells"},{"label":"A","text":"GLUT-4"},{"label":"B","text":"GLUT-2"},{"label":"C","text":"GLUT-5"},{"label":"D","text":"GLUT-3 Cyanide is an inhibitor of oxidative phosphorylation at complex ___acting by binding to _____"},{"label":"A","text":"Complex-IV by binding to cytochrome-c oxidase"},{"label":"B","text":"Complex-V by binding to protons"},{"label":"C","text":"Complex-II binding to cytochrome-c"},{"label":"D","text":"Complex-I by binding to cytochrome-c oxidase Cyanide, oligomycin, and 2,4-dinitrophenol (DNP) are inhibitors of mitochondrial aerobic phosphorylation. Which of the following statements correctly describes the mode of action of the"},{"label":"A","text":"Cyanide and 2,4-dinitrophenol inhibit the respiratory chain, and oligomycin inhibits the synthesis of ATP."},{"label":"B","text":"Cyanide inhibits the respiratory chain, whereas oligomycin and 2,4-dinitrophenol inhibit the synthesis of ATP."},{"label":"C","text":"Cyanide, oligomycin, and 2,4-dinitrophenol compete with O2 for cytochrome oxidase (ComplexIV)."},{"label":"D","text":"Oligomycin and cyanide inhibit synthesis of ATP; 2,4-dinitrophenol inhibits the respiratory chain. Which of the following is the major circulating ketone body during starvation and or diabetes"},{"label":"A","text":"Beta-hydroxybutyrate"},{"label":"B","text":"Acetoacetate"},{"label":"C","text":"Acetone"},{"label":"D","text":"Acetoacryl-coA Almost all metabolic disturbances in alcohol toxicity can be explained by which of the"},{"label":"A","text":"Depletion of NAD+"},{"label":"B","text":"Elevated pyruvate concentration"},{"label":"C","text":"High ATP/AMP ratio"},{"label":"D","text":"Depletion of TCA intermediates What is the use of converting pyruvate to lactic acid during strenuous exercise or in"},{"label":"A","text":"To regenerate NAD+ from NADH so that glycolysis can continue"},{"label":"B","text":"Liver prefers lactic acid for gluconeogenesis"},{"label":"C","text":"Balance of body pH"},{"label":"D","text":"Lactic acid can be converted to glucose via Cori cycle"},{"label":"A","text":"ATP"},{"label":"B","text":"FADH"},{"label":"C","text":"hexokinase"},{"label":"D","text":"acetone Succinate-ubiqunone-oxidoreductase is also known as:"},{"label":"A","text":"Comples-II"},{"label":"B","text":"Complex-IV"},{"label":"C","text":"Complex-V"},{"label":"D","text":"Complex-I The end product of oxidation of odd carbon fatty acids is:"},{"label":"A","text":"Phytanic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"Propionic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"Acetic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Oxaloacetate In terms of the time required to produce ATP energy on oxidation, how would you rank"},{"label":"A","text":"Fatty acids, glucose, amino acids and lastly ethanol"},{"label":"B","text":"Ethanol, amino acids, fatty acids and lastly glucose"},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose, fatty acids, ethanol and lastly amino acids"},{"label":"D","text":"Ethanol, glucose, fatty acids and lastly amino acids During gluconeogenesis which of the following crosses the inner mitochondrial"},{"label":"A","text":"Malic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"Oxaloacetic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"Pyruvic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Citric acid"},{"label":"A","text":"Oxidative phosphorylation"},{"label":"B","text":"Respiratory burst"},{"label":"C","text":"Oxygen transport"},{"label":"D","text":"Glycolysis Excessive carbohydrate catabolism can lead to ketone body formation"},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE Cori cycle dominates during marathon"},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE Alcohol increases acetyl-coA on oxidation thus inhibiting carbohydrate catabolism"},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE Carbon monoxide is an efficient electron transport enhancer"},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE Mobilization of liver glycogen is stimulated mainly by glucagon"},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE Lactate dehydrogenase H4 isoform is found in heart muscles"},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE The reason why erythrocytes do not have nucleus is that a nucleus would occupy too large space for hemoglobin binding and transportation"},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE Because of differences in pyruvate kinase affinities to their substrates the fetus can survive even when maternal blood is hypoglycemic."},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE Although TCA cycle produces 30 net moles of ATP, it is 100 times less efficient than oxidative phosphorylation in efficiency."},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE During fatty acid oxidation the liver secretes ketone bodies in the blood because that is the only way the liver could export metabolic fuel derived from fatty acids to extra- hepatic tissues."},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE Thiamine deficiency in childhood lead to retarded general growth"},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE Serious mutations of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex are usually lethal"},{"label":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"B","text":"FALSE Marching in the missing enzymes in the following diagram of glycolysis"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels","duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","duplicate_option_text","nonunique_option_labels","nonunique_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-61cdb3a1965f","stem":"Which of the following is a feature of apoptosis?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unveri