it is suggested that the following interpreta- tion be given to AUDIT scores: I Scores between 8 and 15 are most appropriate for simple advice focused on the reduction of hazardous drinking. I Scores between 16 and 19 suggest brief counseling and continued moni- toring. HOW TO HELP PATIENTS I 21 How to Help Patients sing the AUDIT to screen patients is only the first step in a process of helping Box 5U reduce alcohol-related problems and risks. Advise PatientsHealth care workers must decide what not to Drinkservices they can provide to patients who score positive. Once a positive case has I When operating a vehicle or been identified, the next step is to provide machinery an appropriate intervention that meets the I When pregnant or considering needs of each patient. Typically, alcohol pregnancy screening has been used primarily to find I If a contraindicated medical“cases” of alcohol dependence, who are condition is presentthen referred to specialized treatment. In recent years, however, advances in screen- I After using certain medications, ing procedures have made it possible to such as sedatives, analgesics, screen for risk factors, such as hazardous and selected antihypertensives drinking and harmful alcohol use. Using the AUDIT Total Score, there is a simple way to provide each patient with an appropri- third level, Zone III, is suggested by AUDIT ate intervention, based on the level of risk. scores in the range of 16 to 19. Harmful and hazardous drinking can be managed While this discussion will focus on helping by a combination of simple advice, brief those patients who score positive on the counseling and continued monitoring, AUDIT, sound preventative practice also with further diagnostic evaluation indicated calls for reporting screening results to if the patient fails to respond or is suspected those who score negative. These patients of possible alcohol dependence. The fourth should be reminded about the benefits of risk level is suggested by AUDIT scor