 Public Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b8482d6f2347","stem":"What makes a state powerful?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5d83e424fa75","stem":"When the Class Representative (CR) told us that we were going to have a session at Uzima Garden, I was very curious. I couldn’t really picture what we would be doing at Uzima Garden – how were we going to learn in a garden?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eb130cffe677","stem":"Define the following terms commonly used in Blood Transfusion","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eceb59f6c751","stem":"List six (6) Blood Donor Health check criteria.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a72a460b8dbf","stem":"List four (4) Advantages and one (1) Disadvantage of Acid Citrate Dextrose Adenine (CPDA)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5a8258945a78","stem":"List five requirements for a Blood transfusion refrigerator or blood bank refrigerators.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Medical Laboratory Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Laboratory Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3ae6b84d171a","stem":"List four (4) types of Investigations (tests) which should be done, before a unit of Blood from the Blood Bank Refrigerator, is transfused to a Patient. (4 marks)","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Medical Laboratory Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Laboratory Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ed0824c2b24c","stem":"Explain on the causes of bleeding disorders","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-586fcab3173c","stem":"What is development? Development has captured the attention of economists and politicians alike. - Everyone is talking about it: The academicians, religious leaders, economists, students, peasants etc. From the rural to urban areas everyone is talking about. What does it mean? From the developed nation everyone is talking about it. What is the meaning of it?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b48af94322b4","stem":"When young, a woman has to obey her father and brothers.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f71de284aae5","stem":"When married, she has to obey her husband.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3886f4175811","stem":"When widowed, she has to obey her son.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ca31408ed2a8","stem":"What is DS and why study it?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The subject area of DS also known as Development Economics is concerned with societal reproduction and transformation process of particularly developing countries"},{"label":"B","text":"The discipline attempts to explain theoretically and empirically the patterns and processes of transformation and stagnation in countries and its implication to health."},{"label":"C","text":"Relevance of DS"},{"label":"D","text":"As a multi disciplinary subject it provides a methodological perspective of viewing development processes taking place in society It attempts to situate professional specializations within concrete social, political and economic conditions prevailing in society."},{"label":"E","text":"Relevance Contd.."},{"label":"F","text":"The ultimate goal is to mould a socially enlightened and responsible professional expert."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"4a68f733932f49242dae6c0eb805de86a8b5e902"},{"id":"q-3a8a3015d9ce","stem":"What is DS and why study it?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"PowerPoint Presentation"},{"label":"B","text":"PowerPoint Presentation"},{"label":"C","text":"PowerPoint Presentation"},{"label":"D","text":"Relevance of DS"},{"label":"E","text":"Relevance Contd.."},{"label":"F","text":"COURSE STRUCTURE"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic","duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","nonunique_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem","subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"4a68f733932f49242dae6c0eb805de86a8b5e902"},{"id":"q-04d542dadd42","stem":"Capitalism: • Two antagonistic classes, the class of bourgeoisie and proletariat. • The major means of production were primarily controlled by bourgeoisie. • The bourgeoisie paid proletariats a little wage which did not sustain their lives. (Is this still going on under multinational capitalism under globalization? Examine how employees are paid by their employers at their places of work, are they adequately paid or not??). • Due to class conflict in the society this led to change the mode to another one.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-fc520ef7fa38","stem":"Socialism: • The World System school provides the socialistic solution to the underdevelopment of Africa, thus; socialism should be adopted by all African states. but • Is it true that socialism provides a better chance of development for African states? • In Tanzania since Independence we adopted socialism, did we developed?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4fd74a6b8d6f","stem":"Zonal economic integration: • e.g. ECOWAS, SADCC, PTA, COMESA, East African States. But will this truly lead to real development?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-58e72cfd26e6","stem":"To cut off the existing relationship with the core: • Is it possible to cut off relationship with the core now that “we call them our development partners?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-769fc1b17c2b","stem":"How is HIV/AIDS spread (transmitted)?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-58647cad6412","stem":"WHAT DO WE DO? DIFFERENT PARTNERS HAS A ROLE TO PLAY GOT/local government Legislation laws and policies DONORS – Funding Local government /NGO’S Implementation at Grassroots level Legislation laws and policies [Slide 3] What is sex? Sex refers to the biological and physiological characteristics of males and females, such as reproductive organs, chromosomes or hormones. It is usually difficult to change. What is Gender? Gender roles, relationships and responsibilities result in different opportunities and limitations for women and men. These roles are hierarchical and privilege one group over another, leading to unequal power relations. What is Gender? Gender roles, relationships and responsibilities result in different opportunities and limitations for women and men. These roles are hierarchical and privilege one group over another, leading to unequal power relations.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Physiology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"E","text":"g. Haemophilia among boys, ovarian cancer among women While these sets of biological characteristics are not mutually exclusive, as there are individuals who possess both; they tend to differentiate humans as males and females [Slide 4] FIGURE 1 [Slide 5] Gender refers to the norms, roles, relationships, activities, responsibilities and attitudes of women and men. It varies from society to society and can be changed."},{"label":"E","text":"g. More road traffic injuries among men, more trachoma among women Gender describes the different expectations, roles, beliefs and behaviors assigned to men and women in a given society. Gender can also be referred to as “masculinity” or “femininity.” Gender, or masculinity and femininity, is based on social, religious, cultural and historical norms about what is deemed appropriate for men and boys, and for women and girls. [Slide 6] What is Gender Gender is socially constructed Socialization Process Gender roles, relationships, and norms Gender stereotypes Gender-based division of labour [Slide 7] The social construct of gender Sex Gender Socialization Gender Norms Gender Roles Gender Relations Gender Stereotypes Gender-based division of labour [Slide 8] Gender Norms – Summary Point There are no “right” or “wrong” gender norms but they lead to inequality if they contribute to: ̈Mistreatment of one sex over the other ̈Differences -in power and opportunities- Gender Norms – Summary Point ̈There are no “right” or “wrong” gender norms but they lead to inequality if they contribute to: ̈Mistreatment of one sex over the other ̈Differences -in power and opportunities- [Slide 9] Gender roles... Are closely related to gender norms Refer to the expected roles that males and females should play in a particular society Are unwritten rules about what males and females are “supposed” to do in households, communities and the workplace Gender relations ... Are established based on gender norms and roles Are a form of social relations that determine how women and men of different ages should interact and behave with one another Contribute to the establishment of power relations [Slide 10] Summary note on gender norms, roles and relations Gender norms, roles and relations are not “bad” in and of themselves. When they produce inequalities between women and men, they may increase their exposure to risk factors or vulnerability to health conditions due to: stereotypes and discrimination gender-based division of labour Gender stereo types ... Images, beliefs, attitudes or assumptions we have about a certain group of people. Usually negative and based on assumptions about a group according to pre-determined roles and norms. [Slide 11] Gender based division of labour ... Refers to formal and informal market activities Includes occupations outside of the home and tasks that men and women do in the community and household (paid or unpaid) Note: Gender norms, roles, relations – and sometimes stereotypes - often influence where women and men conduct their paid and/or unpaid responsibilities. [Slide 12] Concluding thoughts on gender norms, roles and relations Different roles per se are not the cause of inequality; it is the value placed on those roles that lead to inequality. In most societies, a higher value is ascribed to masculine roles. These may also lead to gender stereotypes. Gender norms, roles and relations affect women and men differently. Norms and roles that undervalue women often lead to: Social exclusion Decreased access to important resources to protect their health. Many societal norms encourage men and boys to engage in high-risk behaviors that harm both themselves and others. [Slide 13] Unpacking the difference between Sex and Gender Sex refers to the biological and physiological characteristics of males and females, such as reproductive organs, chromosomes or hormones. It is usually difficult to change."},{"label":"E","text":"g. Haemophilia among boys, ovarian cancer among women Gender refers to the norms, roles, relationships, activities, responsibilities and attitudes of women and men. It varies from society to society and can be changed. Often requires long-term strategies."},{"label":"E","text":"g. More road traffic injuries among men, more trachoma among women [Slide 14] DEFINING GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE ̈The United Nations defines Violence Against Women (VAW) as: \"....any act of gender-based violence that results in, or is likely to result in: Physical, sexual Psychological harm or suffering to men or women, including threats of such acts, coercion or arbitrary deprivations of liberty, whether occurring in public or private life. GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE Gender Based Violence (GBV) is an umbrella term for any harmful act that is perpetrated against a person’s will and that is based on socially ascribed. Generally the risk of facing acts of GBV is higher for women and girls than for men and boys. Especially the subordinate status of women increases their vulnerability to violence [Slide 15] In summary People are born female or male, but are taught what appropriate behaviour and roles are expected of them, including how they should relate to other people. ̈Due to the different value ascribed to these norms and roles and stereotypes, inequality between women and men results. ̈Inequality leads to discriminatory practices. In summary ̈Unlike sex, gender roles and behaviours can change historically, sometimes relatively quickly – but often take long periods of time. [Slide 16] ̈Gender roles and associated behaviours are upheld and protected by societies who consider them to be the “natural order of things” (both men and women). ̈Changing gender norms, roles and relations is often contested and requires short, medium and long term strategies in order to redress issues of inequality. OVERVIEW OF THE GENERAL FORMS OF GBV ̈Both men, women and girls and boys can face different forms of violence throughout their lives. They can be perpetrated either by a women’s partner, family member or person within the general community. [Slide 17] GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE... GBV takes on many forms and can occur throughout the lifecycle, from the prenatal phase through childhood and adolescence, the reproductive years, and old age GENDER -VIOLENCE(GBV) ̈Women and girls are the most at risk and most affected by GBV. Consequently, the terms “violence against women” and “gender-based violence” are often used interchangeably [Slide 18] Physical violence includes beating, hitting, slapping, burning or choking a person FORMS OF GENDER BASED VIOLENCE------ Physical violence includes beating, hitting, slapping, burning or choking a person In a 2009 study 56,2% of women living in the Lake Zone regions of Tanzania report having faced physical violence. [Slide 19] FORMS OF GBV Forms of GBV include harmful traditional practices such as: Early and forced marriage, Honour killings, Female genital cutting; Child sexual abuse and slavery; Trafficking in persons; sexual coercion and abuse; neglect; domestic violence; and Elder abuse. [Slide 20] FORMS OF GENDER BASED VIOLENCE ̈47,8% of women living in the Lake Zone regions report having ever faced sexual violence. ̈Sexual violence is used at home, but also at work, in schools, and in public places. FORMS OF GENDER BASED VIOLENCE------ ̈Physical violence against men, women, girls, boys and children in general, is often perpetrated by the person who is responsible for caring for them, such as a parent or a caretaker. [Slide 21] FORMS OF GENDER BASED VIOLENCE----- Sexual violence includes forced sex (rape), refusal to practice safe sex or forcing a person to do sexual acts against their will. Sexual violence, sexual abuse and rape are frequently reported in Tanzania [Slide 22] FORMS OF GENDER BASED VIOLENCE Sexual violence includes forced sex (rape), refusal to practice safe sex or forcing a person to do sexual acts against their will. Sexual violence, sexual abuse and rape are frequently reported in Tanzania SPECIFIC FORMS OF GENDER BASED VIOLENCE---1--- ̈There are more specific forms of violence that Tanzanian that occur at certain stages of their lives."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Physiology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-a22db9dae987","stem":"What is Gender?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-812204e0a476","stem":"What is sex?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"pract