:"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incidence"},{"label":"B","text":"Occurrence"},{"label":"C","text":"Frequency"},{"label":"D","text":"Prevalence"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"14ae3d89be262d1424eb2bcb83224153350b9c73"},{"id":"q-f17e17135e99","stem":"The following are measures of disease frequency except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevalence"},{"label":"B","text":"Incidence density"},{"label":"C","text":"Incidence"},{"label":"D","text":"Relative risk"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5dfc6b3f375d","stem":"Select the correct answer","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The odds ratio and relative risk should always approximate one another."},{"label":"B","text":"The odds ratio will tend to overestimate the relative risk unless the disease prevalence is less than 10%."},{"label":"C","text":"The relative risk will tend to overestimate the odds ratio unless the disease prevalence is less than 10%."},{"label":"D","text":"Odds ratios are calculated in cohort studies and relative risks are calculated in case- control studies. 11"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_explanation_mixed_into_stem","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"c91c4262685c62fad2d21b6633bdcf2204ceab1d"},{"id":"q-668ffb725ed3","stem":"Which of the following best describes the retrospective design where subjects are sampled by disease status and is often used when the investigator is interested in rare diseases.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intervention trial"},{"label":"B","text":"Case control study"},{"label":"C","text":"Retrospective cohort"},{"label":"D","text":"Ecology study"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d856e9b3a937","stem":"Which of the following best describes the study design that can be either retrospective or prospective and is often used when the investigators are interested in rare exposures.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intervention trials"},{"label":"B","text":"Cohort studies"},{"label":"C","text":"Prevalence studies"},{"label":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-99a424e938f7","stem":"The strength of an association is one of the criteria for evaluating the cause and effect relationship between an exposure and outcome. Which of the following is a measure of the strength of association? (Choose one best answer).","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incidence rate among the exposed"},{"label":"B","text":"Cumulative incidence among the exposed"},{"label":"C","text":"The ratio of odds of exposure among cases to the odds of exposure among the non-cases"},{"label":"D","text":"Odds of disease among exposed relative to the prevalence of exposure in the source population 12"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"fa4e317fb33b16e274674ae61f40bc34baaebc70"},{"id":"q-8b5a4e3992af","stem":"If we wanted to obtain a measure of the incidence of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro during 2007, what breast cancer cases would we count in the numerator?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Incidence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"All cases of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro in 2007"},{"label":"B","text":"Only newly diagnosed cases of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro in 2007?"},{"label":"C","text":"All women in Kilimanjaro region"},{"label":"D","text":"All women at reproductive age only at Kilimanjaro"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3fedcfe3901436155346573e1cd6b30bfb5a7734"},{"id":"q-0dd1c83c2941","stem":"Assuming that we begin to measure incidence on January 1, 2007, which Kilimanjaro women would be counted for the denominator of the incidence measure?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Incidence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"All women in Kilimanjaro in2007."},{"label":"B","text":"Only women in Kilimanjaro without breast cancer on January 1, 2007."},{"label":"C","text":"All women in Kilimanjaro with and without breast cancer"},{"label":"D","text":"A and B are most correct"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"aed85da9453553e20224b695c5af6e02a9345f08"},{"id":"q-d8f0c7b1094d","stem":"To measure the prevalence of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro for the year 2007, what breast cancer cases would we count in the numerator?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Prevalence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"All breast cancer cases reported to the Kilimanjaro Cancer Registry in 2007"},{"label":"B","text":"All breast cancer cases ever reported to the Kilimanjaro Cancer Registry?"},{"label":"C","text":"All surviving breast cancer cases ever reported to the Kilimanjaro Cancer Registry?"},{"label":"D","text":"A and B are correct"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3e502c12c23fc35750be0eb2367b9e9502b33d6f"},{"id":"q-54ea7191a774","stem":"For which of the following disease situations could a cross-sectional study design be used to determine prevalence?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Prevalence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disease w, a highly fatal disease in which the average length of survival after diagnosis is approximately one month."},{"label":"B","text":"Disease x, a disease with a long latency period where infection is identifiable by a serological test."},{"label":"C","text":"Disease y, a disease which is caused by a chromosomal malformation present at birth (or before)."},{"label":"D","text":"Both b and c 13"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"847ba617be6193536452717232b0f6d501bb97a8"},{"id":"q-76b5bc3059af","stem":"A hypothetical study of the effect of alcohol intake on the risk of gallstones in Tanzanians aged 20-74 was conducted. Ultrasonography of the gallbladder of 2200 study subjects identified 152 subjects with gallstones, 42 of whom listed their alcohol consumption level as \"high\". Of the men without gallstones, 220 listed their alcohol intake level as \"high\". Interviews were conducted at the same time as the examinations to determine the exposure level of study participants. Which measure of association is most appropriate in this cross-sectional design?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevalence ratio"},{"label":"B","text":"Prevalence odds ratio"},{"label":"C","text":"Cumulative incidence"},{"label":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ef825357d89e67e0fa65fde357291dc6a690f03c"},{"id":"q-1807e56f678f","stem":"What happens to the prevalence of a disease in a population when the average duration of the disease increases?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Prevalence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"it increases"},{"label":"B","text":"it decreases"},{"label":"C","text":"it stays the same"},{"label":"D","text":"it depends with disease"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"23b63ba51736330a257a0d30fbfaf8b6b83495bf"},{"id":"q-b0f23444aaec","stem":"One of the following is the characteristic of a cohort study design","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Open population"},{"label":"B","text":"Dynamic population"},{"label":"C","text":"Closed population"},{"label":"D","text":"A and C are correct"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-673f335c5d3d","stem":"What can happen to the size of an open cohort over time?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"May shrink/decrease"},{"label":"B","text":"May grow"},{"label":"C","text":"May remain constant"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above 14"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"d834e661406b1aab230066218cb0f27f48c9b421"},{"id":"q-62149f9686e9","stem":"About cross sectional study design","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"studies are carried out at one time point or over a short period"},{"label":"B","text":"They are usually conducted to estimate the prevalence of the outcome of interest for a given population"},{"label":"C","text":"Commonly for the purposes of public health planning."},{"label":"D","text":"Data can also be collected on individual characteristics, including exposure to risk factors, alongside information about the outcome."},{"label":"E","text":"All above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"354517759930f38d4a727432b09e9febf858b2b9"},{"id":"q-9e4a2bd07b47","stem":"The risk ratio assess","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Risk Ratio","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"How much more likely the exposure is to develop an outcome compared to non exposure"},{"label":"B","text":"How much more likely the non exposure is to develop disease as opposed to the exposure"},{"label":"C","text":"How much risk between the two groups compared"},{"label":"D","text":"How much risk is available when you are not exposed"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8b2dd700d2ef","stem":"Researchers want to assess if there is an association between cigarette smoking and strokes. Of the following, which is the best study design to assess for this association?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". Case series"},{"label":"B","text":". Cross-Sectional study"},{"label":"C","text":". Prospective cohort study"},{"label":"D","text":". Randomized controlled trial"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3c749d60be5914fcf61ee9d775b6c28252a57820"},{"id":"q-c326911186a5","stem":"One important characteristic that distinguishes intervention studies from observation studies is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". observation studies allow you to directly calculate a relative risk"},{"label":"B","text":". observation studies take advantage of natural events"},{"label":"C","text":". observation studies require less time to complete"},{"label":"D","text":". it is easier to control for confounding in observation studies 15"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"4599528381683b1e48cb9ad2b98e77148daf4a21"},{"id":"q-f706fcb987d7","stem":"Which one of the following examples might represent a retrospective cohort study?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subjects with current angina followed for the development of myocardial infarction"},{"label":"B","text":"Subjects with prior radiation exposure followed for the development of lymphoproliferative cancers"},{"label":"C","text":"Subjects randomly assigned to active varicella vaccine versus placebo, followed for the chickenpox"},{"label":"D","text":"Subjects with skin cancer assessed for lifelong cumulative sun exposure"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"512a6deab8f95b48d714e0e43175ca6664d3cfee"},{"id":"q-7f3de702df1d","stem":"Proper hand washing by the staff is a way to reduce hospital acquired (nosocomial) infections. SEM Memorial Hospital decided to implement a hand washing training program, but it was only possible to conduct the program on two of its four medical floors. The staff on two floors volunteered to participate in the training. Several months after the completion of the program on the two floors, the SEM staff decided to conduct a study and prospectively collect nosocomial infection data of all four floors. The nosocomial infections on the two floors that underwent the program were 7/10,000 patient days while there were 12/10,000 patient days on the floors without the training. This study is an example of a","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". case-control study"},{"label":"B","text":". cohort study"},{"label":"C","text":". cross-sectional study"},{"label":"D","text":". randomized controlled trial"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8b216bc93078","stem":"Referring to the scenario in question # 2 above, what measure would you use to assess the impact, expressed in patient days, on nosocomial infections that is due to the hand washing program. Select the best answer.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". relative risk"},{"label":"B","text":". incidence density (rate)"},{"label":"C","text":". cumulative incidence"},{"label":"D","text":". attributable risk 16"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"3694e6b4e3fca94534edbde0af0c65f6d8992e89"},{"id":"q-ffab381a9bb2","stem":"Attributable risk","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". is the amount or proportion of the disease that can be attributed to a specific exposure"},{"label":"B","text":".is a measurement of excess disease if the non-exposed remain non-exposed"},{"label":"C","text":". is the relative risk in the exposed minus the relative risk in the non-exposed"},{"label":"D","text":". is the prevalence of disease in the exposed minus the prevalence of disease in the non-exposed"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3210feea2863c517c81bf37643faec3945d0611e"},{"id":"q-be311be60056","stem":"An advantage of conducting case control studies rather than randomized controlled trials (RCT) is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". Selection bias is less likely to occur in a case control study vs. a RCT."},{"label":"B","text":". A case control study can assess more outcomes vs. a RCT."},{"label":"C","text":". A case control study can determine disease prevalence better than a RCT."},{"label":"D","text":". A case control study is better for studying associations with rare outcomes vs. a RCT"},{"label":"E","text":". Information bias is less likely to occur in a case control study vs. a RCT."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dbc33eba73c8","stem":"All of the following should be considered when assessing causality except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". consistency with other knowledge"},{"label":"B","text":". biologic plausibility"},{"label":"C","text":". disease prevalence"},{"label":"D","text":". strength of association"},{"label":"E","text":". alternative explanations"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-265e6eb81ba6","stem":"A new headache remedy was given to a group of 25 subjects who had headaches. Four hours after taking the new remedy, 20 of the subjects reported that their headaches had disappeared. From this information you conclude","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"That the remedy is effective for the treatment of headaches."},{"label":"B","text":"Nothing, because the sample size is too small."},{"label":"C","text":"Nothing, because there is no control group for comparison."},{"label":"D","text":"That the new treatment is better than aspirin."},{"label":"E","text":"That the remedy is not effective for the treatment of headaches. 17"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"b942f05d85c97e02f6448a543e7d80a80d7436c6"},{"id":"q-f5406e18b087","stem":"The association between induced abortion and breast cancer has been the subject of previous epidemiological studies. Cohort studies have found no association, while at least one case-control study has found a positive association. Possible explanations for the different results in case-control and cohort studies of this topic include (choose single best answer).","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case-control studies are prone to selection bias, whereas cohort studies are not vulnerable to selection bias."},{"label":"B","text":"Recall bias might explain the association observed in a case-control study, but this would not be a problem in prospective cohort studies."},{"label":"C","text":"The method of disease classification is different in case-control and cohort studies."},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f33bf0423401","stem":"All of the following meet the criteria for primary prevention except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetanus vaccination for pregnant women"},{"label":"B","text":"DPT vaccination for infants"},{"label":"C","text":"Pap smear screening"},{"label":"D","text":"Fluorination of water"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-937ac27b55bf","stem":"One of the factors below is important in making decision on which measure of central tendency to use for data summarization. Choose the correct factor.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". Sample size"},{"label":"B","text":". The scale of measurement"},{"label":"C","text":". The study design"},{"label":"D","text":". The sampling method"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-13e3626deddf","stem":"The mean is used more often than the median as a measure of the central tendency because the standard error of the median is approximately 25% larger than the standard error of the mean when the distribution of observations is approximately normal. This means that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". Median has greater variability from one sample to another"},{"label":"B","text":"Median has minimum variance in skewed distribution 18"},{"label":"C","text":"Mean is recommended statistically when the distribution is normal"},{"label":"D","text":"A and B are the most correct"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"0545538ee9b16f0d8dc5e9408a4f08ce4057fdbc"},{"id":"q-8cef4075dcf4","stem":"Which of the following symbols represent the population parameter?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"SD"},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":"r"},{"label":"D","text":"0"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eb2064267f4a","stem":"The goal of .................is to focus on summarizing and explaining a specific set of data","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferential statistics"},{"label":"B","text":"Descriptive statistics"},{"label":"C","text":"None of the above"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above 19 SECTION II (15 MARKS)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"6ccb8e50c187b3ee963e519f28b250ee3fdc9c24"},{"id":"q-934263edb988","stem":"For each of the following epidemiological measures, indicate whether it is a rate, a proportion or that it is neither a rate nor a proportion. (5 points)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Population attributable risk ___________________________"},{"label":"B","text":"Lung cancer mortality per 100, 000 _____________________"},{"label":"C","text":"Prevalence of spinal bifida at KCMC _____________________"},{"label":"D","text":"Relative risk _____________________"},{"label":"E","text":"Cumulative incidence _____________________"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-268704124b2c","stem":"Indicate true or false next to each of the following. (10 points)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"___ A risk ratio measure and a correlation coefficient are both measures of association."},{"label":"B","text":"___ The study base for a case-control study consists of those people who if they developed the disease could have been counted as cases."},{"label":"C","text":"___ The Bradford Hill criterion \"coherence\" means that the association has been observed repeatedly in different places, by different observers, and at different times."},{"label":"D","text":"___ If an exposure is a cause of a disease, then \"temporality\" is the Bradford Hill criterion for causal inference that must hold true between exposure and disease."},{"label":"E","text":"___ The decision to use an incidence density measure or a cumulative incidence as a measure of the strength of association may depend on the objectives of the study. Cumulative incidence is preferred if estimating individual risk is the main objective."},{"label":"F","text":"___ An advantage of cohort designs compared to the pure case control designs is that cohort studies can directly estimate risks."},{"label":"G","text":"___The temporal sequence of exposure and disease can be directly addressed in a cohort design as well as in a case control study."},{"label":"H","text":"___ As an estimate of a relative risk, an odds ratio is a measure of association that can be used to determine the magnitude of an association between exposure and an outcome. 20 i) ___ Case control studies have several crucial advantages that relate to their efficiency for studying rare conditions and those with prolonged induction and their efficiency in examining many exposure and outcomes. j) ___ Cross-sectional studies are limited by their lack of generalizability, but are powerful in that they directly measure risk. SECTION III (60 MARKS)"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","too_many_options"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"5c6a4d998e73d300fe02641d24fe5ae3c2ee2f6c"},{"id":"q-25202b13dd8c","stem":"Study the following biological measures (blood glucose concentration in mmol/L among pregnant women) and answer the following questions: ID number Blood glucose level ID number Blood glucose level Patient 1 6.8 Patient 10 6.0 Patient 2 5.3 Patient 11 7.2 Patient 3 6.1 Patient 12 6.4 Patient 4 4.3 Patient 13 6.0 Patient 5 5.0 Patient 14 5.5 Patient 6 7.1 Patient 15 5.8 Patient 7 5.5 Patient 16 8.8 Patient 8 3.8 Patient 17 4.5 Patient 9 4.6 Patient 18 5.9 3 A. Which level of measurements will you use for the following variables?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Obstetrics and Gynecology","topic":"Antenatal Care · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antenatal Care","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calculate; Mean (2), Median (1), Mode (1)"},{"label":"B","text":"Calculate range (1)"},{"label":"C","text":"Calculate the standard deviation (3)"},{"label":"D","text":"Which scale of measurement was used? (1) 22 For each variable, give the level of measurement and measure of central tendency (MCT) you will use to summarize the data (5 points)"},{"label":"A","text":"Blood haemoglobin level Scale of measurement: MCT:"},{"label":"B","text":"Outcome of malaria treatment Scale of measurement: MCT:"},{"label":"C","text":"Systolic blood pressure among adults Scale of measurement: MCT:"},{"label":"D","text":"Severity of dysponea Scale of measurement: MCT:"},{"label"