d Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-67fe84a10924","stem":"d. Is range or standard deviation a better measure of the distribution in this case and why?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f8340f2f52cf","stem":"system? (4 pts)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5da0f6e0962b","stem":"c. What is the positive predictive value?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-85f1cab2d211","stem":"interpret this value? (1 pts)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9a7af5711407","stem":"likely to underestimate or overestimate the true number of work-related fatal injuries? (1","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0f585b661fb1","stem":"SECTION I - MULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS Choose the most correct answer 1. A median is: a) the average of the group the middle number of the group the most common number of the group a measure of the spread in a group a and b are correct 2. Consider the following values of a continuous variable 15 17 12 16 48 What is the best way of summarizing these data? the mean and standard deviation the mean and 95% confidence interval the median and the standard deviation the median and interquartile range b and d are correct 3. What is (are) characteristics of the normal distribution? the mean equals the median the distribution has a bell shape curve the standard deviation equals the standard error both answer a and b answer a, b and c 4. Why is it important to determine if a variable is normally distributed? it changes the way you summarize data it changes the way you analyse your data it is absolutely irrelevant both a, b and c all are correct","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_explanation_mixed_into_stem","possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-66bd0119b39c","stem":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"yes\"?>","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"<a:clrMap xmlns:a=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main\" bg1=\"lt1\" tx1=\"dk1\" bg2=\"lt2\" tx2=\"dk2\" accent1=\"accent1\" accent2=\"accent2\" accent3=\"accent3\" accent4=\"accent4\" accent5=\"accent5\" accent6=\"accent6\" hlink=\"hlink\" folHlink=\"folHlink\"/>"},{"label":"B","text":"lï—ÄgÏžlÞMê2q]8"},{"label":"C","text":"“ßÿÿ=,"},{"label":"D","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags €PlaceName"},{"label":"E","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags €PlaceType"},{"label":"F","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d345471c620f51033193346114ce04085734c807"},{"id":"q-38454a4befa9","stem":"Calculate; Mean (2), Median (1), Mode (1) Calculate range (1) Calculate the standard deviation (3) 6 d. Which scale of measurement was used? (1) 3 A. Which level of measurements will you use for the following variables? For each variable, give the level of measurement and measure of central tendency (MCT) you will use to summarize the data (5 points) a) Blood haemoglobin level Scale of measurement: MCT: Outcome of malaria treatment Scale of measurement: MCT: Systolic blood pressure among adults Scale of measurement: MCT: Severity of dysponea Scale of measurement: MCT: Number of hospital visits Scale of measurement: MCT: 3 B. Study the following figure and answer the questions that follow (5 points)","type":"Calculation","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2c03cd2c7302","stem":"Which of the following best describes the similarities and differences in the three distributions shown in the figure above? a) Same mean, median, mode; same standard deviation b) Same mean, median, mode; different standard deviation c) Different mean, median, mode; same standard deviation d) Different mean, median, mode; different standard deviation 28. Interquartile range covers what proportion of a distribution? a) 25% b) 50% c) 75% d) 100% 29. The following method can be used to show the relationship between two continuous variables Histogram Pie chart Scatter diagram Bar chart Line graph 30. Simply by scanning the values in each distribution below, identify the distribution with the largest standard deviation: a) 1, 10, 15, 18, 20, 22, 25, 30, 39 b) 1, 3, 8, 10, 20, 20, 30, 32, 37, 39 c) 1, 15, 17, 19, 20, 20, 21, 23, 25, 39 d) 41, 42, 43, 44, 45, 45, 46, 47, 48, 49 31. In the definition of epidemiology, ‘distribution’ refers to all of the following except: a) Who b) When c) Where d) Why 32. Endemic means that a disease a) Occurs clearly in excess of normal expectancy b) Incidence is constant in specific geographical area c) Affects a large number of countries simultaneously d) Exhibits a seasonal pattern 33. In the definition of epidemiology “determinants” generally includes all of the following except: a) Agents b) Risk factors c) Control measures d) Causes 34. Among ten women with cervical cancer, medical records confirm a past history of herpes simplex type 2 infection in eight. What is the odds of developing cervical cancer in women with a history of HSV-2 type infection? a) 8/10 b) 10/8 c) 8/2 d) 2/10 35. A hallmark feature of an analytic epidemiology study is: a) Laboratory confirmation of the diagnosis b) Use of appropriate comparison group c) Statistical analysis using logistic regression d) Conducting surveillance for communicable diseases 36. The epidemiological triad of disease causation refers to: (chose one best answer) a) Agent, host, environment b) Time, place, person c) Source, mode of transmission, susceptible host d) John Snow, William Farr, John Graunt 37. A pap smear and colposcopic examination for early detection of cervical cancer and papilloma virus infection constitute a) Primary prevention b) Secondary prevention c) Tertiary prevention d) Medical treatment 38. All of the following describe analytical studies except: a) Test hypothesis b) Quantitative and qualitative techniques may be used c) Answers how and why d) Help in establishing causation 39. All of the following best describes the latency period except: a) Used for chronic diseases b) Extend from exposure to disease onset c) It is a stage of subclinical infection d) It can vary from seconds to few days 40. People with Hepatitis A or measles are more infectious: a) During stage of susceptability b) During stage of subclinical disease c) During stage of clinical disease d) During stage of recovery 41. All of the following are character of descriptive studies except: a) Describe occurrence of health event by person, time and place b) Help in generating the hypothesis c) Help in testing the hypothesis d) Can be categorized as observational studies 42. Johns Snow investigation of Cholera is considered a model for epidemiological field investigation because it included all of the following except: a) Biologically plausible hypothesis b) Spot map c) Comparison of a health outcome among exposed and unexposed group d) Multivariate statistical model 43. Disease control measures are generally directed at all of the following except: a) Eliminating the reservoir b) Eliminating the vector c) Increasing host susceptibility d) Increasing host immune response For Q 44 – 50: Which term best describes the pattern of occurrence of diseases 44. Usually 40 – 50 cases per week; last week, 48 cases a) Endemic disease b) Epidemic disease c) Sporadic disease d) Pandemic disease e) Hyper-endemic disease 45. Fewer than 10 cases per year; last week, 1 case a) Endemic disease b) Epidemic disease c) Sporadic disease d) Pandemic disease e) Hyper-endemic disease 46. Usually no more than 2-4 cases per week; last week, 13 cases a) Endemic disease b) Epidemic disease c) Sporadic disease d) Pandemic disease e) Hyper-endemic disease 47. Average annual incidence was 365 cases of gonorrhea per 100,000 population in Moshi, compared with national average of 135 cases per 100,000 population a) Endemic disease b) Epidemic disease c) Sporadic disease d) Pandemic disease e) Hyper-endemic disease 48. Over 33 million people globally are infected with HIV a) Endemic disease b) Epidemic disease c) Sporadic disease d) Pandemic disease e) Hyper-endemic disease 49. 22 cases of measles occurred in a district within 3 weeks among underfive children (usually 3 cases per year) a) Endemic disease b) Epidemic disease c) Sporadic disease d) Pandemic disease e) Hyper-endemic disease 50. About 60 cases of congenital syphilis are usually reported in this district per year, sli","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bda057fe7ddb","stem":"a normal distribution a skewed (not-normal) distribution both a normal and a skewed distribution both answer a and b could be correct, it is not possible to say not-normal distribution, but left skewed distribution 13. Fill in the missing words to the quote: ‘‘statistical methods may be described as methods for drawing conclusions about......................... Based on...................................computed from the............................’’ Statistics, samples, populations Populations, parameters, samples Parameters, statistics, populations Populations, statistics, samples 14. A study was conducted to measure the effect of smoking upon the birth weight of a baby. The following variables were measured for each woman in the study: smoking status (yes or no), baby weight (gm), Apgar score, birth date. The scales of these variables are: Nominal, ratio, ratio, ratio Ordinal, ratio, interval, interval Nominal, ratio, ordinal, interval Ordinal, ratio, ordinal, ordinal 15. Descriptive statistics is used to characterize the set of data in terms of: a. Shape (Graphs, charts) b. Tables (Frequency distribution tables) c. Central tendency, Tables, Graph, charts, dispersion d. Variability 16. For the following histogram, what is the proper ordering of the mean, Median and mode?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-930d05f23197","stem":"00 PM INSTRUCTIONS TO CANDIDATES 1. Write your examination Code Number on each examination sheet and never your name. 2. Write answers on the examination sheets provided for multiple choice questions and for other questions use space provided in each question. 3. This is a Three hours paper consisting of Twenty printed pages with three sections. Section I : Multiple Choice Questions Section II : True/False Questions Section III : Short Notes Questions 4. Read each question carefully. 5. Attempt all Questions SECTION: I MULTIPLE CHOICE QUESTIONS Choose the most correct answer 1: About Hypothesis testing The null hypothesis means that there is no difference The smaller the P value (less than 0.05) the more likely is our data, and the greater the evidence to reject the null hypothesis The smaller the P value (less than 0.05) the more likely is our data, and the greater the evidence to accept the null hypothesis The study hypothesis means that there is difference 2: In order for the project/research/study to be feasible It must consider resources (time and money) There is agreement of the meaning of the research question Whether everything that needs to be measured can be measured All of the above 3: Which one of the following examples might represent a retrospective cohort study? Subjects with current angina followed for the development of myocardial infarction Subjects with prior radiation exposure followed for the development of lymphoproliferative cancers Subjects randomly assigned to active varicella vaccine versus placebo, followed for the chickenpox Subjects with skin cancer assessed for lifelong cumulative sun exposure 4: Randomization and double blinding in a controlled trial are intended to accomplish all of the following except Minimise bias Optimize external validity-(An expression of the degree to which a test is capable of measuring what is intended to measure.) Reduces or eliminate known confounders None of the above 5: All of the following study designs are useful for hypothesis generation except Randomised controlled field trials Retrospective cohort studies Longitudinal ecologic studies Case-control studies 6: The nonrandom sampling type that involves selecting a convenience sample from a population with a specific set of characteristics for your research study is called. a) Convenience sampling b) Quota sampling c) Purposive sampling d) Snowball sampling 7: If we took the 500 people attending a school in New York City, divided them by gender, and then took a random sample of the males and a random sampling of the females, the variable on which we would divide the population is called the a) Dependent variable(Outcome variable)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Stratification variable"},{"label":"C","text":"Sampling variable d) Frame sampling 8: Which of the following is not an example of a nonrandom sampling technique? a) Purposive b) Quota c) Convenience d) Cluster 9: As a member of Public health team, you have an interest in controlling measles infection through vaccination, to estimate the level of immunity in your study population and derive the data useful for generating vaccination policy. You should a) Conduct a case control study of measles infection b) Conduct randomised trial of measles vaccination c) Conduct a retrospective cohort study of measles vaccination d) Conduct cross sectional study of vaccination status 10: Cross sectional surveys are subject to the Neyman bias, or late look bias. This may be explained as a) The tendency to detect only the late stages of a disease, when manifestations are more severe b) The tendency to detect preferentially the more indolent cases of a disease c) The tendency to detect only those cases of a disease that are asymptomatic d) The tendency to detect preferentially fatal disease 11: Case finding relies on the a) Identification of the people with known risk factor b) Identification of the people with known disease c) Identification of the people with early stages of disease d) None of the above 12: Sampling and sample size a) Randomisation is the best way to control bias b) Randomisation is the best way to control selection bias c) Representative sample selected through random sampling technique is the best way for generalizing the results d) b and c are most correct 13: Select the correct answer. Randomization: a) Helps to avoid misclassification of exposure status in randomized controlled trials. b) Reduces the probability of selection bias in randomized controlled trials. c) Helps to avoid the necessity of performing an adjustment. d) Helps to avoid interaction 14: When questioned years after an illness, cases reported more severe subjective symptoms than did controls. This is probably an example of: a) Misclassification b) Random error c) Confounding d) Recall bias 15: A study was conducted to estimate the effectiveness of doing assignments in an introductory statistics course. Students in one section taught by instructor A received no assignments, Students in another section taught by instructor B, received assignments. The final grade of each student was recorded. A 95% confidence interval for the difference in the mean grades (Section A - Section B) was computed to be"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_explanation_mixed_into_stem","answer_text_in_option","long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-778c65a07b06","stem":"List Paragraph","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Header Char"},{"label":"B","text":"Header"},{"label":"C","text":"TUMAINI UNIVERSITY KILIMANJARO CHRISTIAN MEDICAL COLLEGE END OF MID SEMESTER 4 EXAMINATIONS SUBJECT : EPIDEMIOLOGY (EPI - 203) PROGRAMME : DOCTORS OF MEDICINE (MD) YEAR : MD II DATE : 5/6/2008 TIME : 2.00"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["short_or_incomplete_stem"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","short_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b67a6738bad9","stem":"own words 69. Open-ended questions provide primarily ______ data. Confirmatory data Qualitative data Predictive data None of the above 70. Which of the following is not a form of nonrandom sampling?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0ca0079eee5c","stem":"representation of the population from which a sample has been taken? A large sample based on the convenience sampling technique A small sample based on simple random sampling A large sample based on simple random sampling A small cluster sample 72. Sampling in qualitative research is similar to which type of sampling in quantitative research? Simple random sampling Systematic sampling Quota sampling Purposive sampling 73. Select the","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_explanation_mixed_into_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["answer-leak or footer removed"]},{"id":"q-42a05da19137","stem":".. 3. Ethics is the set of principles and guidelines that help us to uphold the things we value..T... 4: Consider the following research example: Out of 14 patients you have treated with a particular drug, three suffered from particular side effects. The proportion is 3/14 = 0.2143 what can you say about the probability of a complications in the entire population of patients who will be treated with this drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". The issue of random sampling"},{"label":"B","text":". 5: Gender may be a confounder even if the percentage of females in each study arm is comparable"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-158f3686dca8","stem":".. SECTION III: SHORTS NOTES QUESTIONS Q: 1 Read the scenario and answer questions that follow (15 marks) BMI is a measure of obesity (weight / height2). A study set out compare BMI between men and women. Mean BMI in a sample of men was 26.8 and mean BMI in a sample of women was 29.0. The difference between the mean for women and the mean for men was 2.2, with a 95% confidence interval from 0.1 to 4.3. The P value for this difference was calculated to be 0.048 (i.e. P = 0.048). What would the null hypothesis be? There is no difference between men and women mean BMI What would the study hypothesis be? The women have higher BMI compared to men What was the objective of this study? To compare BMI between men and women Name the measure central tendency used in this study Mean e) Explain why do you think this measure of central tendency was used instead of others Probably because the distribution of the weight and height was normally distributed f) What would you comment on the difference observed, in terms of Magnitude- Was 2.2 with 95% CI: 0.1 to 4.3 Direction- Women has higher BMI by 2.2 than men Statistical","type":"Calculation","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6a8506da3135","stem":"Source: data from Abdullah et al (2001) Questions: 2 a) Based on the information of this study, suggest the title for this table? Malaria prevalence to children of 2yrs old in Tanzania 2 b) i: Was this study descriptive or analytical? Analytical ii: Name the study design used in this survey Prospective Cross-sectional 2 c) Does it matter that data from some of the eligible children were not included in the analysis?-YES 2 d) Describe the results of the study shown in the table above focus on the number of net ownership and number of children with parasitaemia across years","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Prevalence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b457d372c0a7","stem":"2 e) Restrict yourself to year 1997 and 1999; what is the prevalence ratio for the effect of owning a treated net on the prevalence of parasitaemia?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Prevalence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-81448fea36b8","stem":"Q: 3 A study was conducted by a police traffic officer from the existing records in the police station from his report he first stated that 10% of all road accidents are due to drunken driving, and before allowing question from the audience he over emphasized the seriousness of the accidents and lastly concluded that it is logical to state that 90% of all remaining road accidents are due to driving without drinking (10 marks) 3: a) Suggest type of study design used here Cross sectional 3: b) Do you think this is the correct way of presenting data (Figures)?","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-900fab989d0a","stem":"NO ii) Defend your choice Does not provide temporal association between cause and outcome Because it is conducted at one point in time, can not predict trends over time Can not estimate incidence of disease iii) Suggest by writing the way the figure would have been presented Report as cohort study(Total no.of cases followed over time,no of drunkers,no.of non-drunkers and compare the axposure-outcome association) 3: c) Comments on the percentages reported on his first and second statements. Do you think it was appropriate information to the stakeholders?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Incidence","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"SEQ Figure \\* ARABIC"},{"label":"B","text":"INCLUDEPICTURE \"http://www.umanitoba.ca/centres/mchp/protocol_external/images/methods_design_case_control.jpg\" \\* MERGEFORMATINET"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b3a2d75ad889","stem":"The definition of confounding requires that the potential confounder must be an independent risk factor for the exposure .. 7: Recall bias is most commonly problem in retrospective cohort .. 8: Publication bias is the same as reporting bias .. 9: Broad objectives is similar to aim of the study .. 10: Randomization is aiming at making the result of the study generalisable .. SECTION III: SHORTS NOTES QUESTIONS Q: 1 Read the scenario and answer questions that follow (15 marks) BMI is a measure of obesity (weight / height2). A study set out compare BMI between men and women. Mean BMI in a sample of men was 26.8 and mean BMI in a sample of women was 29.0. The difference between the mean for women and the mean for men was 2.2, with a 95% confidence interval from 0.1 to 4.3. The P value for this difference was calculated to be 0.048 (i.e. P = 0.048). What would the null hypothesis be? .. What would the study hypothesis be? What was the objective of this study? . Variables from this study Q: 2 Read the scenario and respond to questions below (15 marks) In 1997 investigator carried out a cross sectional study of children 2 years of age in 18 villages in Tanzania at the beginning of a marketing campaign to promote the use of treated nets. They collected data on net ownership and other factors by questionnaire (the mothers of children were interviewed), and took blood samples from the children in the study to assess whether they were infected with malaria parasites (parasitaemia), and also to see whether they were anaemic (as a consequence). The children were also assessed for clinical signs of malaria. Two further surveys were done over the next 2 years. A different random sample of children was selected on each occasion. The investigator identified 985 eligible children, and interviewed the mothers of 827 of these children over the course of three cross sectional surveys (16 mother refused consent, and 142 could not be contacted). Of the 827 interviewees, data from 748 were included in the analysis because 68 children were aged over 2 years at the time of sampling and net status was not known for 11 children. Table 1 below show some of the results from this study Table: 1 Source: data from Abdullah et al (2001) Questions: 2 a) Based on the information of this study, suggest the title for this table? .. 2 b) i: Was this study descriptive or analytical? 2 e) Restrict yourself to year 1997 and 1999; what is the prevalence ratio for the effect of owning a treated net on the prevalence of parasitaemia? Q: 3 A study was conducted by a police traffic officer from the existing records in the police station from his report he first stated that 10% of all road accidents are due to drunken driving, and before allowing question from the audience he over emphasized the seriousness of the accidents and lastly concluded that it is logical to state that 90% of all remaining road accidents are due to driving without drinking (10 marks) 3: a) Suggest type of study design used here","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"D","text":"Name the measure central tendency used in this study"},{"label":"E","text":"Explain why do you think this measure of central tendency was used instead of others . f) What would you comment on the difference observed, in terms of Magnitude Direction Statistical . g) Name independent .and dependent Year of survey No of children eligible No of children analyzed No (%) ownership No net Untreated net Treated net No (%) of children With anemia With parasitaemia With splenomegally . ii: Name the study design used in this survey . 2 c) Does it matter that data from some of the eligible children were not included in the analysis?........................................................................ . 2 d) Describe the results of the study shown in the table above focus on the number of net ownership and number of children with parasitaemia across years"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-69d142839d17","stem":".. 3. Ethics is the set of principles and guidelines that help us to uphold the things we value..... 4: Consider the following research example: Out of 14 patients you have treated with a particular drug, three suffered from particular side effects. The proportion is 3/14 = 0.2143 what can you say about the probability of a complications in the entire population of patients who will be treated with this drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". The issue of random sampling"},{"label":"B","text":". 5: Gender may be a confounder even if the percentage of females in each study arm is comparable"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bbb32763cb35","stem":"Source: data from Abdullah et al (2001) Questions: 2 a) Based on the information of this study, suggest the title for this table?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eda624461814","stem":".. 2 b) i: Was this study descriptive or analytical?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ee3fcb32d6b","stem":". 2 c) Does it matter that data from some of the eligible children were not included in the analysis?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-afbb4c28bac9","stem":"Name the type of variable indicated in this table.............................. 2: What is the total percentage of ex-smokers?....................................... 3: What will be the proportion of the never smokers?............................... 4: Name the two graphical presentations that are possible from this table .................................... .................................... 5: A survey in which thick smears are performed on all of the villagers in one village shows that 14 per cent are positive. Does this represent an incidence rate or a prevalence rate?...................................................... 6: In January there were 50 cases of malaria diagnosed in District X (population 200 000). Of these cases, 30 were males and 20 females. Question: What proportion of the cases was in males?............................... Questions 7–9 Read the scenario below and by the help of the table constructed respond to questions (7 – 9) Scenario: A psychiatrist devised a short screening test for depression. An independent blind comparison was made with a gold standard for diagnosis of depression among 200 psychiatric outpatients. Among the 50 outpatients found to be depressed according to the gold standard, 35 patients were positive for the test. Among 150 patients found not to be depressed according to the gold standard, 30 patients were found to be positive for the test.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Psychiatry","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depression"},{"label":"B","text":"Total"},{"label":"C","text":"Present"},{"label":"D","text":"Absent"},{"label":"E","text":"Test positive"},{"label":"F","text":"negative"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Psychiatry","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-991550106d82","stem":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"yes\"?>","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"<a:clrMap xmlns:a=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main\" bg1=\"lt1\" tx1=\"dk1\" bg2=\"lt2\" tx2=\"dk2\" accent1=\"accent1\" accent2=\"accent2\" accent3=\"accent3\" accent4=\"accent4\" accent5=\"accent5\" accent6=\"accent6\" hlink=\"hlink\" folHlink=\"folHlink\"/>"},{"label":"B","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags"},{"label":"C","text":"€place"},{"label":"D","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags"},{"label":"E","text":"Ôu1⁄4"},{"label":"F","text":"4§òJÿ"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","nonunique_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"d345471c620f51033193346114ce04085734c807"},{"id":"q-965d38a7e2fc","stem":"What does it mean when you calculate a 95% confidence interval? a. The process you used will capture the true parameter 95% of the time in the long run b. You can be “95% confident” that your interval will include the population parameter c. You can be “5% confident” that your interval will not include the population parameter d. All of the above statements are true 34. What would happen (other things equal) to a confidence interval if you calculated a 99 percent confidence interval rather than a 95 percent confidence interval? a. It will be narrower b. It will not change c. The sample size will increase d. It will become wider 35. Which of the following statements sounds like a null hypothesis? a. The coin is not fair b. There is a correlation in the population c. There is no difference between male and female incomes in the population d. The defendant is guilty 36: As a general rule, researchers tend to use ____ percent confidence intervals. a. 99% b. 95% c. 50% d. none of the above 37. Which of the following is the researcher usually interested in supporting when he or she is engaging in hypothesis testing? a. The alternative hypothesis b. The null hypothesis c. Both the alternative and null hypothesis d. Neither the alternative or null hypothesis 38. When p<.05 is reported in a journal article that you read for an observed relationship, it means that the author has rejected the null hypothesis (assuming that the author is using a significance or alpha level of .05). a. True b. False 39. When p>05 is reported in a journal article that you read for an observed relationship, it means that the author has rejected the null hypothesis (assuming that the author is using a significance or alpha level of .05). a. True b. False 40. _________ are the values that mark the boundaries of the confidence interval. a. Confidence intervals b. Confidence limits c. Levels of confidence d. Margin of error 41. _____ results if you fail to reject the null hypothesis when the null hypothesis is actually false. a. Type I error b. Type II error c. Type III error d. Type IV error 42. A good way to get a small standard error is to use a ________. a. Repeated sampling b. Small sample c. Large sample d. Large population 43 : Identify which of the following steps would not be included in hypothesis testing. a. State the null and alternative hypotheses b. Set the significance level before the research study c. Eliminate all outliers d. Obtain the probability value using a computer program such as SPSS e. Compare the probability value to the significance level and make the statistical decision 44. A ________ is a range of numbers inferred from the sample that has a certain probability of including the population parameter over the long run. a. Hypothesis b. Lower limit c. Confidence interval d. Probability limit 45. ________ is the standard deviation of a sampling distribution. a. Standard error b. Sample standard deviation c. Replication error d. Meta error 46. An effect size indicator is a statistical measure of the strength of a relationship. a. True b. False 47: The cutoff the researcher uses to decide whether to reject the null hypothesis is called the: a. Significance level b. Alpha level c. Probability value d. Both a and b are correct 48: __________ is the failure to reject a false null hypothesis. a. Type I error b. Type II error c. Type A error d. Type B error 49. Which of the following statements is/are true according to the logic of hypothesis testing? a. When the null hypothesis is true, it should be rejected b. When the null hypothesis is true, it should not be rejected c. When the null hypothesis is false, it should be rejected d. When the null hypothesis is false, it should not be rejected e. Both b and c are true 50: A Type I error is also known as a ______. a. False positive b. False negative c. Double negative d. Positive negative 51. A Type II error is also known as a ______. a. False positive b. False negative c. Double negative d. Positive negative 52. If a finding is statistically significant one must also interpret the data, calculate an effect size indicator, and make an assessment of practical significance. a. True b. False 53. The p-value used in statistical significance testing should be used to assess how strong a relationship is. For example, if relationship A has a p=.04 and relationship B has a p=.03 then you can conclude that relationship B is stronger than relationship A. a. True b. False SHORT NOTES QUESTIONS 1: Twelve people were admitted to a district hospital in January 2007 with cerebral malaria. Their ages were as follows: (Without showing calculations, indicate only the answer in the space provided) 8, 1, 64, 2, 3, 2, 4, 1, 2, 6, 13, 3. a) What is the range of ages in years? nswer: 3 d) In this case, is the mean or median the best measure of the distribution of th óãÓóãÆ1ó ̄ó ̄ó ̄ó¥ó¥ó¥ó›Žó ̄ó ̄ó ̄ó ̄ó ̄ó ̄ó ̄ó›1 ̄ó€ós ̄ óéóéóÜÏÜÁÜÏÜ°°Ü íÜíÜË·Ë·Ë·Ë·Ë·Ë·Ë¦’¦ êÙÈ·È·£·£··£·£· `„ ̃þgd]>? ëÚÇÚ¶ÚëÚ©Ú ̃©ˆwc© ̃R ̃RA `„|ügdj-1⁄4 ðãÓãÂ±Â¤–¤†¤†¤†¤±uhXhXhXhXhXh „ ̃þ^„h `„ ̃þgd9Kw ïÛïÎ1⁄2¥{j]O]j]?]1 „ ̃þ^„h `„ ̃þgd}1¦ ñàñÒñàñÅ ̧ÅaÅšÅ ̧Š ̧yey ̧T ̧G ̧ „ ̃þ^„h `„ ̃þgd}1¦ ñäÔäÇ·Ç·Ç·Ç·ÇäaTMˆwˆiYiL ðãðãðãðãðãÒãÐãÒ¿2¥ ̃ e population? Select one only. °Ð/ °à=!° °Ð/ °à=!° °Ð/ °à=!° °Ð/ °à=!° [Content_Types].xml¬‘ËjÃ0 E÷...þƒÐ¶Øro(¥ØÎ¢Iw},Ò ä±-j„4 Éßwì ̧Po “óTéU^h...d}ã ̈«ôûî)»×*1P ƒ'¬ô “^××Wåî","type":"Calculation","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"quarantine","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"quarantine","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-f7e77243011c","stem":"s: (a) There is evidence that doing assignments improves the average grade because the difference in the population means is less than zero. (b) There is little evidence that doing assignments improves the average grade because the 95% confidence interval does not cover 0. (c) There is evidence that doing assignments improves the average grade because the 95% confidence interval does not cover 0. (d) There is evidence that doing assignments does not improve the average grade because the 95% confidence interval does not cover 0. 6: A variable that is presumed to cause a change in another variable is called a(n): a. categorical variable b. dependent variable c. independent variable d. intervening variable 7: Which of the following can best be described as a categorical variable? a. age b. annual income c. grade point average d. religion 8: In research, something that does not \"vary\" is called a. variable b. method c. constant d. control group 9: Systematic error is associated with: a. Reliability b. Validity 10: An ordinal scale is used to rank order people, objects, or characteristics. a. True b. False 11. Which scale is the simplest form of measurement? a. Nominal b. Ordinal c. Interval","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6aa48daf77c7","stem":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"yes\"?>","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"<a:clrMap xmlns:a=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main\" bg1=\"lt1\" tx1=\"dk1\" bg2=\"lt2\" tx2=\"dk2\" accent1=\"accent1\" accent2=\"accent2\" accent3=\"accent3\" accent4=\"accent4\" accent5=\"accent5\" accent6=\"accent6\" hlink=\"hlink\" folHlink=\"folHlink\"/>"},{"label":"B","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags"},{"label":"C","text":"€place"},{"label":"D","text":"\\î?þ"},{"label":"E","text":"8\"ÿ"},{"label":"F","text":"`„ ̃þ‡h"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"d345471c620f51033193346114ce04085734c807"},{"id":"q-a1a6160f4ded","stem":"A parent calls the local Department of Public Health concerned that several children in his small community have been diagnosed with myocarditis, an inflammation of the heart muscle. After recording the information, one of the very first things the public health official should do is: (Select the best answer.) a.) Gather information from sources in the small community to determine if there is a greater than expected number of cases of myocarditis. b.) Conduct active surveillance by capturing ticks from area woods to determine the prevalence of the bacteria that causes Lyme Disease. c.) Conduct a case control study to determine if cases were more likely than controls to have eaten in the nearby fast-food restaurant. d.) Conduct a prospective cohort study to determine if exposure to wooded areas is associated with the development of myocarditis. e.) In the interest of public safety, immediately inform the media that there may be an epidemic of a potentially serious disease. 21. Height is normally distributed in Town A. Researchers randomly select 50 subjects from Town A and calculate their mean height and its 95% confidence interval to make an inference about the true average height in Town A. Select the best statement: a.) Confounding could lead to an erroneous conclusion about Town A’s true average height. b.) The sample size of 50 is too small to calculate a 95% confidence interval on the mean. c.) If the researchers had drawn a sample size of 100 rather than 50, the 95% confidence interval around the mean would be narrower. d.) It is not appropriate to use a 95% confidence interval on the sample mean to make an inference about the true mean of Town A. e.) Interviewer bias could lead to an erroneous conclusion about Town A’s true average height. 22. The standard error of the mean is: (Select the best statement.)","type":"Calculation","subject":"Community Health and Pub